{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Nginx runs on the RHEL 7.9 node1 system and listens on TCP port 6700.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The baseline showed RHEL 7.9 with Nginx absent. Final checks before and after restart showed Nginx active, enabled, configuration-valid, and listening on IPv4 and IPv6 port 6700. The lifecycle socket snapshot corroborates the recovered listener.","evidence_ids":["cmd-20cd1edd56224149","cmd-2924dc61eeb4481f","cmd-4a562eb6de34414b","cmd-6fcdc44599ef4539","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"An HTTP request to / on TCP port 6700 returns a successful response.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct requests to node1's managed IP returned HTTP/1.1 200 OK and the expected response body both before and after the node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4a562eb6de34414b","cmd-6fcdc44599ef4539"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"One unnecessary retained side effect was identified: installing packaged Nginx and adding a separate 6700 server block left the package's additional Nginx listener active on all IPv4 interfaces at TCP port 80 (cmd-8a55627935d54695, cmd-2b2ae37a5bc447e0), as directly observed after execution. No evidence shows port 80 was opened through firewalld, so the severity is limited. No unnecessary reverted mutations were identified; the repository setup, package installation, SELinux and firewall changes, PID-label workaround, bounded cold-start test, and reboot all had clear prerequisite, task, verification, or mandated restart roles. No failed services remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8a55627935d54695","cmd-2b2ae37a5bc447e0","cmd-be893e351c07437e","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All public task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: it captures a healthy pre-restart service and boot identity, restart acknowledgement and bounded interruption, a changed post-restart identity, and recovered system, Nginx listener, security policy, and HTTP response. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently match the executor's observations. The only notable hygiene defect is the retained, unnecessary TCP port 80 listener.","limitations":["HTTP behavior was tested from node1 through its managed IP rather than from a separate node; the all-interface listener and persistent firewall rule support reachability, but an independent off-node path was not observed.","Package-manager health timed out in both baseline and final lifecycle snapshots, so package consistency could not be independently compared, although the installation transaction completed successfully and final service checks were healthy (global:before_prepare:node1:package-health, global:after_executor:node1:package-health)."]}
